<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!--
  ==================================================================
  ORGANISM — the same two laminae, joined by a KEY gate

  This is a second BODY over the same two laminae, not a second seam
  on the first one.  Rule 6 forbids two gates feeding one attribute,
  and lamina_respiratory declares exactly one place a value may land
  (CO_ref), so the way to compare two gate policies on that seam is to
  declare two bodies and open whichever one you want to watch:

      GET  /api/organism?organism=organism_key.xml
      ws   /ws/organism?organism=organism_key.xml

  Nothing else in the project changes to run it.  No lamina is edited,
  no Python is touched, no controller is registered.  The gate policy
  lives entirely in this file, which is the point being demonstrated as
  much as the physiology is.

  ==================================================================
  WHY A KEY RATHER THAN A THRESHOLD

  organism.xml watches ONE number: cardiac output's distance from what
  the respiratory lamina is assuming.  That is the right instrument for
  "has this value gone wrong yet".

  It is the wrong instrument for "can this value still be trusted",
  because those are different questions and the model can answer the
  second before the first.  Measured on pump_state's normalised vector,
  tail of 40 cycles:

    state                       CO      HR      EDV     SV
    healthy tachycardia (140)   0.525   0.625   0.350   0.271
    failing pump   (HR 150,     0.576   0.688   0.025   0.114
                    EDV 55)

  CO and HR are nearly IDENTICAL in those two rows.  A threshold gate
  watching cardiac output cannot tell a fit heart working hard from a
  failing one compensating, because on the axis it watches they look
  the same.  What separates them is the shape across all four
  coordinates: filling has collapsed (EDV 0.350 -> 0.025), stroke volume
  with it (0.271 -> 0.114), and the rate has gone up to hold the product
  steady.  That is a PATTERN, and recognising a pattern is what a key
  gate is for.

  So this gate opens on the state that says the assumption is no longer
  safe, rather than waiting for the number to leave the band.  Once it
  is open the seam is live, so when compensation fails and output does
  fall, respiration sees it on the cycle it happens instead of after the
  deviation gate notices.

  ==================================================================
  HOW THE KEY WAS SET

  Every number below is measured, not chosen.

  The reference point is the failing-pump state above, rounded:
  EDV 0.03, SV 0.11, HR 0.70, CO 0.58.

  The weights say which coordinates carry the discrimination.  EDV and
  SV get 1.0 because they are what actually separate the two states.
  HR gets 0.6: it is part of the picture but it is high in healthy
  tachycardia too.  CO gets 0.3, and its low weight is the honest part —
  it barely distinguishes anything here, and including it at full weight
  would let a coincidental output match pull the key open.

  Scored against every state the model can be driven into (match =
  1 - weighted distance, tail of 40 cycles):

    rest (HR 72)                 0.6892     shut
    bradycardia (HR 35)          0.6286     shut
    high output (HR 200)         0.7498     shut
    healthy tachycardia (140)    0.8128     shut   <- closest non-failing
    hypovolaemia (120, EDV 60)   0.9347     OPEN   <- nearest failing
    failing (HR 160, EDV 50)     0.9741     OPEN
    failing (HR 150, EDV 55)     0.9939     OPEN

  match_threshold = 0.90 sits almost exactly in the middle of the gap
  between 0.8128 and 0.9347 — 0.087 clear of the nearest healthy state
  and 0.035 clear of the mildest failing one.

  release_below = 0.85 is pinned from both sides, which is why it is not
  a taste:

    - it must be ABOVE the highest non-failing match (0.8128), or a gate
      that opened would stay open through healthy tachycardia — a valve
      that never lets go is a wire with extra steps;
    - it must be far enough BELOW 0.90 to stop chatter.  Measured, match
      parked on 0.90 with the live feed's own noise (sd 0.0005), 400
      cycles: one boundary gives 165 transitions, any band of 0.01 or
      more gives 1.

  0.85 clears healthy tachycardia by 0.037 and gives a 0.05 band.

  ==================================================================
  DRIFT

  A static key drifts away from the state it was written for as the
  model, the patient or the calibration moves, and the tempting fix is
  to make the key adaptive.  That is a v2 decision (G7) and it is to be
  made on evidence, so the controller records the distance, an EWMA of
  it, and how far that EWMA has moved from the first evaluation, every
  cycle, from v1.  Read `drift` in the gate's state on the snapshot.
  If it climbs steadily across a run, adaptation has a case.  If it does
  not, it does not.  Measure the drift before correcting the drift.
  ==================================================================
-->
<organism id="bdt_key" version="1.0">

  <name>Default Body (key-gated seam)</name>
  <description>
    The same circulation and respiration, joined at the same seam, but
    the valve opens on a recognised state of the pump rather than on a
    single number leaving its band.
  </description>

  <laminae>
    <lamina id="lamina_circulatory" />
    <lamina id="lamina_respiratory" />
  </laminae>

  <permeability_gates>

    <gate id="pg_failing_pump"
          from="lamina_circulatory:pump_state"
          to="lamina_respiratory:CO_ref"
          controller="key">

      <description>
        Opens when the pump matches the shape of a failing one — filling
        collapsed, stroke volume with it, rate up to hold the product
        steady. Cardiac output is nearly unremarkable in that state,
        which is exactly why a threshold on cardiac output cannot see it.
      </description>

      <!-- The controller reads the whole weighted vector; the transfer
           still carries exactly one attribute. Recognising a situation
           and sending a number are two jobs, and only the second one
           puts a value into the other lamina. -->
      <extract attribute="CO" />

      <key metric="weighted_distance"
           match_threshold="0.90"
           release_below="0.85">
        <weight attribute="EDV" ref="0.03">1.0</weight>
        <weight attribute="SV"  ref="0.11">1.0</weight>
        <weight attribute="HR"  ref="0.70">0.6</weight>
        <weight attribute="CO"  ref="0.58">0.3</weight>
      </key>

      <!-- Same transfer settings as the deviation seam, deliberately:
           the two bodies differ in WHEN the valve opens and in nothing
           else, so anything you observe between them is the controller
           and not the plumbing. -->
      <transfer mode="proportional"
                rate="0.2"
                time_delay="0"
                on_close="hold"
                confidence="0.8" />
    </gate>

  </permeability_gates>

</organism>
